<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
		Bins Out has no developer-operated backend, analytics SDK, advertising SDK,
		account system, tracking domain, postcode lookup, location access, or
		third-party runtime dependency.

		NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes is intentionally empty. Apple defines "collect"
		as transmitting data off the device in a way that allows the developer or
		the developer's third-party partners to access it beyond servicing the
		request in real time. Every flow in this app was assessed against that
		definition and none of them meet it:

		  - UPRN to Bristol. The user-entered UPRN is POSTed to Bristol City
		    Council's NextCollectionDates endpoint with no account, device, or user
		    identifier alongside it. Bristol is the user's own waste authority and
		    the source of the schedule, not a partner of this developer, and the
		    developer never receives or has access to the request or any log of it.
		    This is a publisher decision recorded on 2026-08-16; the flow itself is
		    disclosed in full in PRIVACY.md regardless of how it is classified here.
		  - App Group files (property, schedule, settings, widget/Siri payload)
		    never leave the device.
		  - The selected property syncs through the user's own synchronizable
		    iCloud Keychain. Apple operates that transport end-to-end encrypted and
		    the developer has no access to it.
		  - EventKit events are written into a calendar the user selects. Any onward
		    sync is performed by the user's own calendar account, not by this app.
		  - Live Activities are requested with pushType: nil, so ActivityKit state
		    stays on device.
		  - Notifications are local; no push tokens or remote notifications.

		NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes is intentionally empty: neither this target nor
		the widget extension calls any API in the file-timestamp, system-boot-time,
		disk-space, active-keyboard, or user-defaults required-reason categories.
		Persistence uses FileManager.fileExists(atPath:), Data(contentsOf:), and
		Data.write(to:options:), none of which are required-reason APIs, and the
		project uses no UserDefaults or @AppStorage anywhere.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array/>
</dict>
</plist>
